            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/4E/4E5127409B300792644DAC8BCB87F42A/dvd4OyTA.jpeg",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Transition metals, located in the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block of the periodic table, have partially filled "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals and exhibit a wide range of reactivity. Highly reactive metals like iron are crucial for construction and manufacturing, while nearly inert metals like platinum are used in jewelry and catalytic converters."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3736183745",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Extracting these metals from ores depends on their concentration and the difficulty of reduction. Transition metals oxidize in air, react with halogens to form halides, and react with acids to produce salts and hydrogen gas, important in industrial processes. Their compounds, such as oxides and carbonates, are often basic and form stable salts."
              }
            ],
            "id": "173387676",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "These metals can exist in various oxidation states, enabling diverse chemical reactivity. This versatility makes them essential in catalysis, electronics, and new material development, demonstrating their importance in both industrial applications and everyday life."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2298356399",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
